  :adr/related "adr-2609062800-the-input-area-is-an-editor-not-a-readline adr-2609072600-the-repository-brings-its-own-slash-commands"
  :adr/body "## What was asked

Owner instruction, 2026-09-07, against a screenshot of Claude Code's `/` menu:
slash を入れた時点で autocomplete、候補を表示、選択できるように.

## Where it lives

In `itonami-editor`, next to the key semantics, because that is what it is: a
list is only useful if the arrows reach it, and the arrows already mean two
other things.

`candidates` is a function of the buffer and a command list carried in the
editor state, so the whole thing -- what opens the list, what narrows it, what
each key does while it is up -- is testable without a registry or a terminal.
The registry is handed in once at startup; it does not change after that.

## When the list is open

While the caret is inside the first word of a one-line buffer that starts with
`/`. Once a space is typed the operator has moved on to arguments, and a list
that keeps covering the screen while they type them is a list that has to be
dismissed rather than one that helps.

Matching is prefix first, then substring. A prefix is what the operator is
typing: `/st` offers `/status` and `/steer` before `/timestamps`, which has
`st` in the middle. The substring half is not decoration -- it is how `/pin`
finds `/west-pin-advance`, and it is also why `/west-pin-advance` appears at
the END of `/st`.

## What the keys do while it is up

- **↑ ↓** move the selection. Everywhere else Up means history or the line
  above; over a half-typed command name history would throw the name away and
  there is no line above a one-line `/word`. It stops at the ends rather than
  wrapping.
- **Tab** completes to the selection.
- **Enter** completes a partial name, and SENDS one that is already whole.
  Completing `/help` to `/help ` and asking for a second Enter would be the
  menu getting in the way of the commonest case there is.
- **Esc** dismisses the list, and it stays dismissed until the word changes --
  otherwise it comes back on the next keystroke and the key looks broken.

A query that matches nothing shows no list, and Enter then sends the line, so
the operator gets the server's own answer rather than a menu that ate the key.

## On the screen

The list is rows of the block, between the rule and the input, so it reads as
belonging to what is being typed. Same reason as the progress line and the
streamed tail: a list drawn as lines of its own has to be told where the block
is, and disagrees with it the moment either moves.

Eight candidates are shown and the rest are COUNTED (`+53 more — 続けて入力する
と絞り込みます`). A list that silently stops at eight teaches an operator that
the ninth command does not exist.

The status bar names the keys that are live while the list is up (`↑↓ 選択 /
tab · enter 補完 / esc 閉じる`) instead of the ones the menu has taken.

## Measured

On a pty against this workspace, where the registry is 61 commands: `/` shows
eight with `+53 more`; `/we` narrows to one; ↓ then Tab completes to
`/west-pin-advance ` and closes the list.

Four controls, each failing the check it breaks by name: arrows kept meaning
history; Enter always completing and never sending; the list cut at eight
without saying so; and -- from the first run -- four expectations of mine that
were wrong about `/west-pin-advance` matching `/st`, which the tests caught
before the code did.
"}]
